speechtext:
That is very true;
I remember bringing the matter to the attention of my hon. friend when one of these deputations was in Ottawa, and I remember the excuse that was given for the non-establishment of a parcel post system. However, I merely wish to say that this is only the first of the many good things this

Government will grant to the people living in the rural districts and to the consumers of Canada. I want to see them go one step further; I want to see them establish trunk telephone and telegraph lines in Canada, so that the different provinces of this Dominion will be connected by, and the people throughout the length and breadth of this land will obtain the benefits of a telephone and telegraph system established in the post offices of the Dominion. Thus will it be made evident that this is a progressive Government, and that they are not afraid to undertake the establishment of^ beneficial means of communication. I sincerely hope that the day is not far distant when this country will he connected with the Motherland by a Government-owned cable system, the chief features concerning which I have not time to bring up and discuss during the present session. I do not wish to take up the time of the committee further than to express my most hearty approval of the legislation that is now proposed, to congratulate the Government upon the inauguration of this system, and to commend the Postmaster General for the amount of energy he has put forth in establishing this great boon for the benefit of the people.

The hon. member for South Oxford (Mr. Sutherland) will not mind,_ I am sure, if I do not follow him in his delving into ancient provincial history. Reference has been made to applications made by the Ontario Government for subsidies in years gone by. It is true that such applications have been made, and I plead guilty to having been a supporter of the then Prime Minister who asked for the subsidies. At the time of the construction of this road it was problematical what would be the result;
Mr. MACLEAN (S. York).
it was being constructed merely to open up a portion of the country and there was very strong ground for asking for a subsidy. Conditions are somewhat different to-day, but I go so far as to say now that I am not going to oppose the granting of a subsidy to this railway. I want to point out, however, that no political consideration can be charged against the present leader of the Opposition, because it was a friend of his who was then at the head of the Ontario Government and who asked for the subsidy. The present leader of the Opposition has always taken the ground as outlined by him to-night; he declined the request of the Hon. George W. Ross, now Sir George W. Ross, when he was Prime Minister of Ontario, for a subsidy for this road, so that whatever his attitude in that respect was, it certainly was not political.
